{
  "format_version": 2,
  "engine_version": "0.1.0",
  "name": "Pulse sorter",
  "description": "Finale of the circuits: three ions, launched at t = 0, 30 and 60, fly through three pairs of deflection plates, and each top plate hangs on its own circuit with its own power supply. The first charges through a resistor, the second is switched on at t = 40, the third rings with an inductor. Each ion meets the three plates at a different stage of their circuits (the Circuits panel shows each plate's V(t), with the launches marked). Set the three supplies so that every ion lands in its own detector.",
  "reality": {
    "represents": "Timed deflectors: sorting ions by their circuits' timing",
    "remarks": [
      {
        "kind": "kept",
        "line": "Each circuit has its own V(t)",
        "detail": "A plate charging through R, one switched on, one ringing through L: each ion meets each plate at a different stage, as fast deflectors in a beam line do."
      },
      {
        "kind": "simplified",
        "line": "Ideal lumped circuits",
        "detail": "Resistors, capacitors, inductors and switches are ideal; the leads have no resistance, inductance or delay, and the circuit acts on the plate as if the field spread instantly (quasi-static, here exact: the motion is Newtonian)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "simplified",
        "line": "One particle at a time",
        "detail": "The shots fly separately and never meet, as in a weak beam; in an intense beam the particles repel each other (the Beams arc shows it)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "simplified",
        "line": "Motion in one plane",
        "detail": "Everything is symmetric about the plane z = 0, so the particle never leaves it. A real beam also has a width across the plane, where the same fields act differently (focusing in the plane can defocus across it)."
      }
    ]
  },
